---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PaginationController from '../../components/PaginationController.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Spring Boot @Transactional — Deep Dive · Hand Notes"
  description="Complete deep dive into @Transactional: AOP proxy internals, propagation, isolation levels, rollback rules, common pitfalls, and real-world patterns."
>

<!-- ═══ COVER ═══ -->
<div class="cover">
  <div class="cover-subject">Spring Boot · Deep Dive Series</div>
  <h1>@Transactional Annotation and Transaction Management</h1>
  <div class="cover-sub">From AOP proxies to propagation — everything you need to master transactions</div>
  <div class="cover-tags">
    <span class="tag tag-g">🔄 Propagation</span>
    <span class="tag tag-b">🔒 Isolation</span>
    <span class="tag tag-o">🛡️ AOP Proxy</span>
    <span class="tag tag-p">⚡ Rollback Rules</span>
    <span class="tag tag-g">📖 readOnly</span>
    <span class="tag tag-b">⏱️ Timeout</span>
  </div>
  <div class="cover-doodle">🔁</div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 1: The Problem ═══ -->
<div class="page green-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 01</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">💡</span>
    <span class="section-title">1. The Problem @Transactional Solves</span>
    <span class="section-num">01</span>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text">
    <p>Before Spring, managing database transactions manually was <span class="hl">verbose and error-prone</span>. You had to handle BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK, and connection cleanup yourself.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">The Old Way (Pre-Spring / JDBC)</h2>
  <pre class="code-block orange-left"><span class="cl">Connection</span> conn = <span class="kw">null</span>;
<span class="kw">try</span> &#123;
    conn = dataSource.getConnection();
    conn.setAutoCommit(<span class="kw">false</span>);                    <span class="cm">// Begin transaction manually</span>

    <span class="cm">// Business logic</span>
    <span class="cl">PreparedStatement</span> ps1 = conn.prepareStatement(<span class="st">"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ?"</span>);
    ps1.setDouble(<span class="st">1</span>, <span class="st">500.0</span>);
    ps1.setInt(<span class="st">2</span>, <span class="st">1</span>);
    ps1.executeUpdate();

    <span class="cl">PreparedStatement</span> ps2 = conn.prepareStatement(<span class="st">"UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + ? WHERE id = ?"</span>);
    ps2.setDouble(<span class="st">1</span>, <span class="st">500.0</span>);
    ps2.setInt(<span class="st">2</span>, <span class="st">2</span>);
    ps2.executeUpdate();

    conn.commit();                                 <span class="cm">// Commit manually</span>
&#125; <span class="kw">catch</span> (<span class="cl">Exception</span> e) &#123;
    <span class="kw">if</span> (conn != <span class="kw">null</span>) conn.rollback();             <span class="cm">// Rollback manually</span>
    <span class="kw">throw</span> e;
&#125; <span class="kw">finally</span> &#123;
    <span class="kw">if</span> (conn != <span class="kw">null</span>) conn.close();               <span class="cm">// Close manually</span>
&#125;</pre>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink3)">Problems: Boilerplate everywhere, easy to forget rollback, hard to manage nested transactions, no isolation control per method.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">The Spring Way</h2>
  <pre class="code-block green-left"><span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">BankService</span> &#123;

    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>
    <span class="kw">public void</span> transfer(<span class="cl">Long</span> fromId, <span class="cl">Long</span> toId, <span class="cl">BigDecimal</span> amount) &#123;
        accountRepository.debit(fromId, amount);
        accountRepository.credit(toId, amount);
        <span class="cm">// Spring handles BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK automatically</span>
    &#125;
&#125;</pre>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">🎯 Key Insight</div>
    <p>Spring wraps your method with an <strong>AOP proxy</strong>. YOU write business logic. Spring handles the transaction plumbing — begin, commit, rollback, and connection management.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="insight green-ins" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="insight-label">✅ What @Transactional Gives You</div>
    <p>Declarative transaction management · Automatic rollback on exceptions · Configurable isolation levels · Propagation control for nested calls · Read-only optimization · Timeout protection</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 2: AOP Proxy Internals ═══ -->
<div class="page orange-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 02</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">🔬</span>
    <span class="section-title">2. How @Transactional Works — AOP Proxy</span>
    <span class="section-num">02</span>
  </div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">⚠️ Most Important Concept</div>
    <p>When Spring sees <code>@Transactional</code>, it does NOT modify your class. Instead, it creates a <strong>proxy object</strong> that wraps your class. If you don't understand this, you WILL run into mysterious bugs.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">What You Think Is Happening</h2>
  <div class="diag-wrap">
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-box fb-gray">Caller<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">Your code</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arr">→</div>
      <div class="flow-box fb-orange">BankService<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">Your class directly</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink3);text-align:center;margin-top:8px">❌ Direct call to your class — <strong>WRONG assumption!</strong></p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">What ACTUALLY Happens</h2>
  <div class="diag-wrap">
    <div class="phase-flow">
      <div class="phase-box ph-gray">
        <div class="ph-num">Step 1</div>
        <div class="ph-title">Caller</div>
        <div class="ph-sub">Calls bankService.transfer()</div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
      <div class="phase-box ph-orange">
        <div class="ph-num">Step 2</div>
        <div class="ph-title">BankService$$SpringCGLIB$$Proxy</div>
        <div class="ph-sub">Spring-generated proxy intercepts the call</div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
      <div class="phase-box ph-blue">
        <div class="ph-num">Step 3</div>
        <div class="ph-title">BEGIN TRANSACTION</div>
        <div class="ph-sub">Proxy starts DB transaction</div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
      <div class="phase-box ph-green">
        <div class="ph-num">Step 4</div>
        <div class="ph-title">Real BankService.transfer()</div>
        <div class="ph-sub">Your actual business logic executes</div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
      <div class="phase-box ph-purple">
        <div class="ph-num">Step 5</div>
        <div class="ph-title">COMMIT or ROLLBACK</div>
        <div class="ph-sub">Success → commit · Exception → rollback</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--green);text-align:center;margin-top:8px">✅ Proxy wraps your class — handles transaction lifecycle automatically</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">Key Internal Components</h2>
  <div class="diag-wrap">
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-box fb-orange">TransactionInterceptor<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">AOP advice</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arr">→</div>
      <div class="flow-box fb-blue">PlatformTransactionManager<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">begin / commit / rollback</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arr">→</div>
      <div class="flow-box fb-green">DataSource<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">DB connection</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight orange-ins" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="insight-label">🔑 Key Insight</div>
    <p>The proxy reads <code>@Transactional</code> attributes (isolation, propagation, timeout) and passes them to <code>PlatformTransactionManager</code>. Common implementations: <code>JpaTransactionManager</code>, <code>HibernateTransactionManager</code>, <code>DataSourceTransactionManager</code>.</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 3: Self-Invocation Problem ═══ -->
<div class="page red-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 03</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">🐛</span>
    <span class="section-title">3. The Self-Invocation Problem</span>
    <span class="section-num">03</span>
  </div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">💀 Critical Bug Pattern</div>
    <p>Because Spring uses a PROXY, calling <code>@Transactional</code> methods from within the <strong>SAME class</strong> bypasses the proxy completely. The transaction is <strong>NEVER started</strong>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">The Bug</h2>
  <pre class="code-block red-left"><span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">OrderService</span> &#123;

    <span class="kw">public void</span> placeOrder(<span class="cl">Order</span> order) &#123;
        <span class="cm">// ⚠️ PROBLEM: This call goes directly to the real method</span>
        <span class="cm">// It BYPASSES the proxy → NO TRANSACTION is started!</span>
        processPayment(order);
    &#125;

    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>  <span class="cm">// This will be IGNORED when called from placeOrder()!</span>
    <span class="kw">public void</span> processPayment(<span class="cl">Order</span> order) &#123;
        paymentRepository.save(order.getPayment());
        inventoryRepository.reduce(order.getItems());
    &#125;
&#125;</pre>

  <div class="two-col" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title r">Internal Call (BROKEN)</div>
      <pre class="code-block" style="font-size:12px;margin:0"><span class="cm">this.processPayment()</span>
   ↓
OrderService (real)
   placeOrder()
      └─▶ this.process() <span class="cm">← Direct!</span>
          <span class="cm">(NO transaction)</span></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title g">External Call (WORKS)</div>
      <pre class="code-block" style="font-size:12px;margin:0"><span class="cm">injectedBean.processPayment()</span>
   ↓
OrderService$$Proxy
   processPayment() <span class="cm">← Proxy!</span>
   <span class="cm">(TRANSACTION STARTS)</span></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">The Fix — Inject Self</h2>
  <pre class="code-block green-left"><span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">OrderService</span> &#123;

    <span class="an">@Autowired</span>
    <span class="kw">private</span> <span class="cl">OrderService</span> self;  <span class="cm">// Inject self → forces proxy usage</span>

    <span class="kw">public void</span> placeOrder(<span class="cl">Order</span> order) &#123;
        self.processPayment(order);  <span class="cm">// ✅ Goes through proxy now</span>
    &#125;

    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>
    <span class="kw">public void</span> processPayment(<span class="cl">Order</span> order) &#123;
        paymentRepository.save(order.getPayment());
    &#125;
&#125;</pre>

  <div class="insight orange-ins" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="insight-label">💡 Alternative Fixes</div>
    <p><strong>1)</strong> Move the <code>@Transactional</code> method to a separate service class · <strong>2)</strong> Use <code>ApplicationContext.getBean()</code> · <strong>3)</strong> Use AspectJ weaving instead of proxies (compile-time weaving)</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 4: All Attributes ═══ -->
<div class="page blue-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 04</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">⚙️</span>
    <span class="section-title">4. @Transactional Attributes Overview</span>
    <span class="section-num">04</span>
  </div>

  <pre class="code-block blue-left"><span class="an">@Transactional</span>(
    propagation    = <span class="cl">Propagation</span>.REQUIRED,         <span class="cm">// How to join/create transactions</span>
    isolation      = <span class="cl">Isolation</span>.DEFAULT,            <span class="cm">// Isolation level</span>
    readOnly       = <span class="kw">false</span>,                        <span class="cm">// Read-only optimization</span>
    timeout        = <span class="st">-1</span>,                            <span class="cm">// Transaction timeout in seconds</span>
    rollbackFor    = &#123;&#125;,                           <span class="cm">// Exception types that trigger rollback</span>
    noRollbackFor  = &#123;&#125;,                           <span class="cm">// Exception types that do NOT rollback</span>
    transactionManager = <span class="st">"transactionManager"</span>      <span class="cm">// Which TX manager to use</span>
)</pre>

  <h2 class="sub">Attribute → Database Concept Mapping</h2>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Maps to Database Concept</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>isolation</code></td><td>SQL Isolation Levels (ACID - Isolation)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>propagation</code></td><td>Nested/Chained Transaction Behavior</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>readOnly</code></td><td>Read-only transaction hint → optimization</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>timeout</code></td><td>Statement/lock timeout</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rollbackFor</code></td><td>ROLLBACK trigger rules</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>noRollbackFor</code></td><td>COMMIT despite exception</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>transactionManager</code></td><td>Which DataSource / DB to use</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="cheat-grid" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--green);border-top-color:var(--green)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">🔄 propagation</div>
      <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink2)">REQUIRED (default)<br />REQUIRES_NEW<br />NESTED · SUPPORTS<br />MANDATORY · NEVER</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--blue);border-top-color:var(--blue)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">🔒 isolation</div>
      <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink2)">DEFAULT<br />READ_UNCOMMITTED<br />READ_COMMITTED<br />REPEATABLE_READ<br />SERIALIZABLE</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--orange);border-top-color:var(--orange)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">⚡ rollbackFor</div>
      <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink2)">RuntimeException → auto<br />Checked → NO rollback!<br />Use rollbackFor=Exception.class</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--purple);border-top-color:var(--purple)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">📖 readOnly</div>
      <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink2)">Skip dirty checking<br />Can route to replica<br />Hibernate optimization</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 5: Isolation Levels ═══ -->
<div class="page purple-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 05</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">🔒</span>
    <span class="section-title">5. Isolation Levels — Database Meets Spring</span>
    <span class="section-num">05</span>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text"><p>Isolation is about <span class="hl">protecting concurrent transactions from each other</span>. Spring's <code>Isolation</code> enum maps directly to database isolation levels.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">Spring Isolation Enum → Anomaly Protection</h2>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Spring Isolation</th><th>Dirty Read</th><th>Non-Repeatable</th><th>Phantom</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>READ_UNCOMMITTED</code></td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>READ_COMMITTED</code></td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>REPEATABLE_READ</code></td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--orange)">Possible*</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SERIALIZABLE</code></td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>DEFAULT</code></td><td colspan="3">Uses DB's default (MySQL=REPEATABLE_READ)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink3)">* InnoDB prevents phantoms at REPEATABLE_READ via gap locks</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">How to Set Isolation in Spring</h2>
  <pre class="code-block purple-left"><span class="cm">// READ_COMMITTED — Safe from dirty reads (PostgreSQL default)</span>
<span class="an">@Transactional</span>(isolation = <span class="cl">Isolation</span>.READ_COMMITTED)
<span class="kw">public</span> <span class="cl">Order</span> getOrderDetails(<span class="cl">Long</span> id) &#123; ... &#125;

<span class="cm">// REPEATABLE_READ — Consistent reads in same transaction (MySQL default)</span>
<span class="an">@Transactional</span>(isolation = <span class="cl">Isolation</span>.REPEATABLE_READ)
<span class="kw">public void</span> recalculateUserScore(<span class="cl">Long</span> userId) &#123; ... &#125;

<span class="cm">// SERIALIZABLE — Fully isolated, maximum safety, slowest</span>
<span class="an">@Transactional</span>(isolation = <span class="cl">Isolation</span>.SERIALIZABLE)
<span class="kw">public void</span> auditFinancialReport(<span class="cl">Long</span> reportId) &#123; ... &#125;</pre>

  <div class="insight" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="insight-label">🎯 Isolation Decision Guide</div>
    <p><strong>Simple reads (dashboards):</strong> READ_COMMITTED · <strong>Read-then-write same row:</strong> REPEATABLE_READ · <strong>Aggregate queries driving decisions:</strong> SERIALIZABLE · <strong>High-throughput reads:</strong> READ_COMMITTED + optimistic locking</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 6: Propagation Part 1 ═══ -->
<div class="page green-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 06</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">🔄</span>
    <span class="section-title">6. Propagation — REQUIRED &amp; REQUIRES_NEW</span>
    <span class="section-num">06</span>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text"><p>Propagation defines <span class="hl">what happens when a @Transactional method calls another @Transactional method</span>. This is THE most nuanced attribute and the most common source of bugs.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">PROPAGATION.REQUIRED (Default)</h2>
  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">📌 REQUIRED</div>
    <p>Join an existing transaction if one exists. If not, create a new one. This is the <strong>default</strong> and covers ~80% of use cases.</p>
  </div>

  <pre class="code-block green-left"><span class="cm">// orderService.placeOrder() starts TXN-1</span>
<span class="cm">// paymentService.process() JOINS TXN-1 (no new TXN)</span>
<span class="cm">// If payment throws → BOTH rolled back</span>

<span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">OrderService</span> &#123;
    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>  <span class="cm">// propagation = REQUIRED by default</span>
    <span class="kw">public void</span> placeOrder(<span class="cl">OrderRequest</span> req) &#123;
        <span class="cl">Order</span> order = orderRepo.save(<span class="kw">new</span> <span class="cl">Order</span>(req));
        paymentService.processPayment(order);   <span class="cm">// Joins SAME TXN</span>
        inventoryService.reduceStock(order);    <span class="cm">// Joins SAME TXN</span>
        <span class="cm">// All three share ONE transaction. One fails = ALL roll back. ✅</span>
    &#125;
&#125;</pre>

  <h2 class="sub">PROPAGATION.REQUIRES_NEW</h2>
  <div class="sticky" style="transform:rotate(0.3deg)">
    <div class="sticky-label">📌 REQUIRES_NEW</div>
    <p>Always create a BRAND NEW transaction. <strong>Suspend the current one</strong> if it exists. TXN-1 and TXN-2 are completely independent.</p>
  </div>

  <pre class="code-block orange-left"><span class="cm">// Classic use case: Audit logging that must persist even if main TX rolls back</span>
<span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">OrderService</span> &#123;
    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>
    <span class="kw">public void</span> placeOrder(<span class="cl">OrderRequest</span> req) &#123;
        <span class="kw">try</span> &#123;
            <span class="cl">Order</span> order = orderRepo.save(req);
            paymentService.chargeCard(order);   <span class="cm">// This throws!</span>
        &#125; <span class="kw">catch</span> (<span class="cl">PaymentException</span> e) &#123;
            <span class="cm">// Main transaction will rollback</span>
            <span class="cm">// But we STILL want the audit log saved!</span>
            auditService.logFailure(req, e);    <span class="cm">// REQUIRES_NEW → saves in own TXN</span>
            <span class="kw">throw</span> e;
        &#125;
    &#125;
&#125;

<span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">AuditService</span> &#123;
    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>(propagation = <span class="cl">Propagation</span>.REQUIRES_NEW)
    <span class="kw">public void</span> logFailure(<span class="cl">OrderRequest</span> req, <span class="cl">Exception</span> e) &#123;
        <span class="cm">// Runs in TXN-2, completely independent</span>
        <span class="cm">// Even if TXN-1 rolls back, this audit log SURVIVES ✅</span>
        auditLogRepo.save(<span class="kw">new</span> <span class="cl">AuditLog</span>(req, e.getMessage()));
    &#125;
&#125;</pre>

  <div class="insight orange-ins" style="margin-top:12px">
    <div class="insight-label">⚠️ Warning</div>
    <p>REQUIRES_NEW uses a <strong>SEPARATE database connection</strong>. Under high load, this can exhaust your connection pool.</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 7: Propagation Part 2 ═══ -->
<div class="page orange-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 07</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">🔄</span>
    <span class="section-title">7. Propagation — NESTED &amp; Others</span>
    <span class="section-num">07</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">PROPAGATION.NESTED</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Create a <span class="hl-blue">savepoint</span> in the current transaction. If inner fails, rollback to savepoint only — outer continues.</p></div>

  <pre class="code-block blue-left"><span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">OrderService</span> &#123;
    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>
    <span class="kw">public void</span> placeOrder(<span class="cl">OrderRequest</span> req) &#123;
        <span class="cl">Order</span> order = orderRepo.save(req);   <span class="cm">// Must succeed</span>
        <span class="kw">try</span> &#123;
            notificationService.send(order);  <span class="cm">// Nice to have, can fail</span>
        &#125; <span class="kw">catch</span> (<span class="cl">NotificationException</span> e) &#123;
            log.warn(<span class="st">"Notification failed, order is fine"</span>);
            <span class="cm">// Savepoint rolled back, order is safe ✅</span>
        &#125;
    &#125;
&#125;

<span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">NotificationService</span> &#123;
    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>(propagation = <span class="cl">Propagation</span>.NESTED)
    <span class="kw">public void</span> send(<span class="cl">Order</span> order) &#123;
        emailQueueRepo.enqueue(<span class="kw">new</span> <span class="cl">EmailTask</span>(order));
        <span class="cm">// If this throws, only this savepoint rolls back</span>
    &#125;
&#125;</pre>

  <div class="insight" style="margin-top:12px">
    <div class="insight-label">NESTED vs REQUIRES_NEW</div>
    <p><strong>REQUIRES_NEW:</strong> Truly independent (separate connection) · <strong>NESTED:</strong> Sub-transaction within SAME connection (savepoint). If OUTER rolls back → NESTED also rolls back.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">All 7 Propagation Types — Summary</h2>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Propagation</th><th>Existing TXN</th><th>No Existing TXN</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>REQUIRED</code> ⭐</td><td>Join it</td><td>Create new</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>REQUIRES_NEW</code></td><td>Suspend, create new</td><td>Create new</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NESTED</code></td><td>Create savepoint inside</td><td>Create new</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SUPPORTS</code></td><td>Join it</td><td>Run without TX</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>MANDATORY</code></td><td>Join it</td><td style="color:var(--red)">THROW EXCEPTION</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NOT_SUPPORTED</code></td><td>Suspend, run without</td><td>Run without TX</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NEVER</code></td><td style="color:var(--red)">THROW EXCEPTION</td><td>Run without TX</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 8: readOnly + rollbackFor ═══ -->
<div class="page blue-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 08</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">📖</span>
    <span class="section-title">8. readOnly &amp; Rollback Rules</span>
    <span class="section-num">08</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">readOnly = true — Performance Lever</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Setting <code>readOnly = true</code> tells Spring and the database driver to optimize for reads:</p></div>

  <ul class="note-list">
    <li class="ck"><span class="hl-green">Hibernate:</span> Skips dirty checking (no snapshot comparison)</li>
    <li class="ck"><span class="hl-green">Hibernate:</span> Disables flush on session (no accidental writes)</li>
    <li class="ck"><span class="hl-blue">MySQL:</span> Can route to READ REPLICA (if configured)</li>
    <li class="ck"><span class="hl-blue">Database:</span> Hints optimizer to optimize for reads</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="sub">Gold Standard Pattern — Class Level</h2>
  <pre class="code-block green-left"><span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="an">@Transactional</span>(readOnly = <span class="kw">true</span>)   <span class="cm">// Default: all methods read-only</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">ProductService</span> &#123;

    <span class="kw">public</span> <span class="cl">List</span>&lt;<span class="cl">Product</span>&gt; findAll() &#123; ... &#125;          <span class="cm">// readOnly ✅</span>
    <span class="kw">public</span> <span class="cl">Product</span> findById(<span class="cl">Long</span> id) &#123; ... &#125;        <span class="cm">// readOnly ✅</span>

    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>   <span class="cm">// Override: write operation, readOnly = false</span>
    <span class="kw">public</span> <span class="cl">Product</span> create(<span class="cl">ProductDto</span> dto) &#123; ... &#125;   <span class="cm">// Write ✅</span>

    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>   <span class="cm">// Override: write operation</span>
    <span class="kw">public void</span> delete(<span class="cl">Long</span> id) &#123; ... &#125;             <span class="cm">// Write ✅</span>
&#125;</pre>

  <h2 class="sub">Default Rollback Behavior — KNOW THIS!</h2>
  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">⚠️ Common Bug Source</div>
    <p><strong>RuntimeException</strong> → ROLLBACK (automatic) · <strong>Checked Exception</strong> → COMMIT (NOT rolled back!) · This surprises many developers!</p>
  </div>

  <pre class="code-block orange-left"><span class="cm">// ❌ BUG: IOException does NOT trigger rollback!</span>
<span class="an">@Transactional</span>
<span class="kw">public void</span> importProducts(<span class="cl">File</span> file) <span class="kw">throws</span> <span class="cl">IOException</span> &#123;
    productRepo.saveAll(parser.parse(file));
    archiveService.archive(file);  <span class="cm">// throws IOException → COMMITS anyway!</span>
&#125;

<span class="cm">// ✅ CORRECT: Tell Spring to rollback on IOException</span>
<span class="an">@Transactional</span>(rollbackFor = <span class="cl">IOException</span>.<span class="kw">class</span>)
<span class="kw">public void</span> importProducts(<span class="cl">File</span> file) <span class="kw">throws</span> <span class="cl">IOException</span> &#123; ... &#125;

<span class="cm">// Safest: Rollback on ANY exception</span>
<span class="an">@Transactional</span>(rollbackFor = <span class="cl">Exception</span>.<span class="kw">class</span>)
<span class="kw">public void</span> sensitiveOperation() <span class="kw">throws</span> <span class="cl">Exception</span> &#123; ... &#125;</pre>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 9: Timeout + Event Listeners ═══ -->
<div class="page purple-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 09</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">⏱️</span>
    <span class="section-title">9. Timeout &amp; Transaction Events</span>
    <span class="section-num">09</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">timeout — The Watchdog</h2>
  <pre class="code-block purple-left"><span class="an">@Transactional</span>(timeout = <span class="st">30</span>)  <span class="cm">// 30 seconds</span>
<span class="kw">public void</span> generateReport(<span class="cl">ReportRequest</span> req) &#123;
    <span class="cm">// If this method takes &gt; 30 seconds, Spring throws:</span>
    <span class="cm">// TransactionTimedOutException → transaction is rolled back</span>
    heavyReportEngine.generate(req);
&#125;</pre>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title r">Without Timeout</div>
      <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--ink2)">Transaction acquires locks → gets stuck → holds locks for minutes → other transactions queue up → system grinds to halt 🔥</div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title g">With Timeout</div>
      <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--ink2)">After 30s → TransactionTimedOutException → ROLLBACK → locks released → other transactions proceed ✅</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">@TransactionalEventListener — Post-Commit Actions</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Run code <span class="hl-green">only after the transaction commits</span>. Perfect for sending emails, cache invalidation.</p></div>

  <pre class="code-block green-left"><span class="cm">// Publishing the event</span>
<span class="an">@Service</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">UserService</span> &#123;
    <span class="an">@Autowired</span> <span class="cl">ApplicationEventPublisher</span> eventPublisher;

    <span class="an">@Transactional</span>
    <span class="kw">public</span> <span class="cl">User</span> registerUser(<span class="cl">RegistrationRequest</span> req) &#123;
        <span class="cl">User</span> user = userRepo.save(<span class="kw">new</span> <span class="cl">User</span>(req));
        eventPublisher.publishEvent(<span class="kw">new</span> <span class="cl">UserCreatedEvent</span>(user));
        <span class="cm">// Event is "held" until transaction commits</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> user;
    &#125;
&#125;

<span class="cm">// Handling — fires ONLY after commit</span>
<span class="an">@Component</span>
<span class="kw">public class</span> <span class="cl">UserEventListener</span> &#123;

    <span class="an">@TransactionalEventListener</span>(phase = <span class="cl">TransactionPhase</span>.AFTER_COMMIT)
    <span class="kw">public void</span> handleUserCreated(<span class="cl">UserCreatedEvent</span> event) &#123;
        <span class="cm">// Only fires after the transaction commits</span>
        <span class="cm">// No risk of sending email then DB rolling back!</span>
        emailService.sendWelcomeEmail(event.getUser());
    &#125;
&#125;</pre>

  <div class="insight" style="margin-top:12px">
    <div class="insight-label">TransactionPhase Options</div>
    <p><code>BEFORE_COMMIT</code> → just before commit · <code>AFTER_COMMIT</code> → after success ✅ · <code>AFTER_ROLLBACK</code> → after rollback · <code>AFTER_COMPLETION</code> → after either</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 10: Common Pitfalls ═══ -->
<div class="page red-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 10</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">⚠️</span>
    <span class="section-title">10. Common Pitfalls &amp; Gotchas</span>
    <span class="section-num">10</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">Pitfall 1: @Transactional on Private Methods</h2>
  <pre class="code-block red-left"><span class="an">@Transactional</span>          <span class="cm">// ❌ IGNORED! Proxy can't intercept private methods</span>
<span class="kw">private void</span> saveOrder(<span class="cl">Order</span> order) &#123;
    orderRepo.save(order);
&#125;

<span class="an">@Transactional</span>          <span class="cm">// ✅ Works — public method, proxy can intercept</span>
<span class="kw">public void</span> placeOrder(<span class="cl">OrderRequest</span> req) &#123; ... &#125;</pre>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink3)"><strong>Rule:</strong> <code>@Transactional</code> must be on <code>public</code> methods. Private, protected, and package-private are silently ignored.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">Pitfall 2: Exception Swallowed — No Rollback</h2>
  <pre class="code-block red-left"><span class="an">@Transactional</span>
<span class="kw">public void</span> riskyOperation() &#123;
    <span class="kw">try</span> &#123;
        repo.save(entity);
        externalApi.call();  <span class="cm">// Throws RuntimeException</span>
    &#125; <span class="kw">catch</span> (<span class="cl">Exception</span> e) &#123;
        log.error(<span class="st">"Failed but swallowed"</span>, e);
        <span class="cm">// ❌ Exception swallowed → Spring sees no exception → COMMITS!</span>
    &#125;
&#125;

<span class="cm">// ✅ Fix: Rethrow or manually mark for rollback</span>
<span class="kw">catch</span> (<span class="cl">Exception</span> e) &#123;
    <span class="cl">TransactionAspectSupport</span>.currentTransactionStatus().setRollbackOnly();
    <span class="cm">// OR: throw new RuntimeException(e);</span>
&#125;</pre>

  <h2 class="sub">Pitfall 3: LazyInitializationException</h2>
  <pre class="code-block orange-left"><span class="an">@Transactional</span>
<span class="kw">public</span> <span class="cl">Order</span> getOrder(<span class="cl">Long</span> id) &#123;
    <span class="kw">return</span> orderRepo.findById(id).get();
    <span class="cm">// Order.items is LAZY → not loaded yet</span>
&#125;

<span class="cm">// In controller:</span>
<span class="cl">Order</span> order = orderService.getOrder(<span class="st">1L</span>);
<span class="cm">// Transaction is CLOSED here (method returned)</span>
order.getItems().size();  <span class="cm">// ❌ LazyInitializationException!</span></pre>

  <div class="insight orange-ins" style="margin-top:12px">
    <div class="insight-label">💡 Lazy Loading Fixes</div>
    <p><strong>1)</strong> Use <code>JOIN FETCH</code> in query · <strong>2)</strong> Use DTO projection · <strong>3)</strong> Extend transaction boundary · <strong>4)</strong> Set <code>spring.jpa.open-in-view=false</code> and use DTOs</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 11: Decision Guide ═══ -->
<div class="page green-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 11</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">🎯</span>
    <span class="section-title">11. Decision Guide — When to Use What</span>
    <span class="section-num">11</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">Choosing Propagation</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="text-align:center;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)">Is there a calling transaction?</p></div>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title g">✅ YES — Existing Transaction</div>
      <ul class="note-list" style="font-size:14px;margin:0">
        <li class="sq">Share it (default) → <span class="hl-green">REQUIRED</span></li>
        <li class="sq">Be independent (audit/log) → <span class="hl-orange">REQUIRES_NEW</span></li>
        <li class="sq">Partial rollback allowed → <span class="hl-blue">NESTED</span></li>
        <li class="sq">Suspend it, run without → <code>NOT_SUPPORTED</code></li>
        <li class="sq">Must be inside one → <code>MANDATORY</code></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title o">❌ NO — No Transaction</div>
      <ul class="note-list" style="font-size:14px;margin:0">
        <li class="sq">Create a new one → <span class="hl-green">REQUIRED</span></li>
        <li class="sq">Run without transaction → <code>SUPPORTS</code></li>
        <li class="sq">Throw an error → <code>MANDATORY</code> / <code>NEVER</code></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="insight green-ins" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="insight-label">🎯 80% Rule</div>
    <p><strong>REQUIRED</strong> (default) covers ~80% of use cases. Use <strong>REQUIRES_NEW</strong> for audit/logging. Use <strong>NESTED</strong> for partial failures you can recover from.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">Choosing Isolation Level</h2>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Operation Type</th><th>Recommended</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Simple reads (dashboard, stats)</td><td><code>READ_COMMITTED</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reads in multi-step process</td><td><code>REPEATABLE_READ</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Financial (balance check)</td><td><code>REPEATABLE_READ</code> + SELECT FOR UPDATE</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aggregate queries → decisions</td><td><code>SERIALIZABLE</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>High-throughput API reads</td><td><code>READ_COMMITTED</code> + optimistic locking</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 class="sub">Quick Reference</h2>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Config</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Standard service write</td><td><code>@Transactional</code> (defaults)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Read-only query</td><td><code>readOnly = true</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Service class default</td><td><code>@Transactional(readOnly=true)</code> at class level</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Audit / logging</td><td><code>propagation = REQUIRES_NEW</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Checked exception safe</td><td><code>rollbackFor = Exception.class</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Post-commit side effect</td><td><code>@TransactionalEventListener</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 12: Interview Q&A ═══ -->
<div class="page orange-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 12</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">📝</span>
    <span class="section-title">12. Interview Questions &amp; Cheat Sheet</span>
    <span class="section-num">12</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">Common Interview Questions</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li><strong>Q: What does @Transactional do internally?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Spring creates a CGLIB proxy. TransactionInterceptor intercepts calls, uses PlatformTransactionManager to begin/commit/rollback.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: Why doesn't @Transactional work on private methods?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">CGLIB proxies create subclasses. Subclasses can't override private methods. Private @Transactional is silently ignored.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: What is the self-invocation problem?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Calling @Transactional from same class uses <code>this.method()</code> — bypasses proxy, no transaction. Fix: inject self or separate class.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: Default rollback behavior?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">RuntimeException → ROLLBACK. Checked Exception → COMMIT (surprising!). Use <code>rollbackFor=Exception.class</code> to catch all.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: REQUIRES_NEW vs NESTED?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">REQUIRES_NEW = new independent transaction (separate connection). NESTED = savepoint in same connection.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: What does readOnly=true do?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Skips dirty checking, disables flush, can route to replica. It's an optimization hint, not enforcement.</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="sub">Master Cheat Sheet</h2>
  <div class="cheat-grid">
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--green);border-top-color:var(--green)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">🔄 Propagation</div>
      <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink2)">REQUIRED = join/create<br />REQUIRES_NEW = always new<br />NESTED = savepoint</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--blue);border-top-color:var(--blue)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">🔒 Isolation</div>
      <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink2)">READ_UNCOMMITTED<br />READ_COMMITTED<br />REPEATABLE_READ ⭐<br />SERIALIZABLE</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--orange);border-top-color:var(--orange)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">⚡ Rollback</div>
      <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink2)">RuntimeException → auto<br />Checked → NO!<br />rollbackFor=Exception.class</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--red);border-top-color:var(--red)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">🐛 Gotchas</div>
      <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink2)">Private methods ignored<br />Self-invocation bypasses<br />Swallowed exceptions</div>
    </div>
  </div>

